<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<schema xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:diggs="http://diggsml.org/schemas/3"
    xmlns:gml="http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2"
    xmlns:vc="http://www.w3.org/2007/XMLSchema-versioning" vc:maxVersion="1.1" vc:minVersion="1.0"
    elementFormDefault="qualified" targetNamespace="http://diggsml.org/schemas/3" version="3.0.0">

    <!-- Uncomment to clear validation errors without a master file -->
    <include schemaLocation="../Diggs.xsd"/>


    <import namespace="http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2" schemaLocation="gml3.2Profile_diggs.xsd"/>
    <import namespace="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" schemaLocation="xlinks.xsd"/>

    <element name="RigidInclusionSystem" type="diggs:RigidInclusionSystemType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractSamplingFeatureGroup">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A rigid inclusion ground improvement system: the collection of load
                transfer platforms and rigid inclusion columns whose information content is jointly
                interpretable because they were built within one treated area.

                A GROUP, NOT A SAMPLING FEATURE (GR1, 2026-08-22, group-redesign). Reclassified from
                diggs:AbstractPlanarSamplingFeature - the strongest evidence for the reclassification
                is that diggs:AbstractSamplingFeatureType MANDATES referencePoint, and a rigid
                inclusion system has none: it has an extent, not a position, which is exactly what
                diggs:AbstractSamplingFeatureGroupType's own optional extent (SurfacePropertyType) is
                for. Per diggs:AbstractProjectScopedGroup's own definition, a group's content is the
                warrant for interpreting its members jointly - the diggs:RITreatedArea they were
                built within IS that warrant for the columns and platforms.

                THE TREATED AREA AND THE WORKING PLATFORM(S) LIVE ON diggs:RITreatedArea, NOT HERE.
                An earlier version of this documentation said the members "share one working platform
                and one treated area" and that both were properties of this group. They are not, and
                the singular was wrong on the platform: diggs:RITreatedAreaType carries a mandatory,
                REPEATABLE workingPlatform, so one treated area may have been prepared in several
                stages or at several levels. What was flat on this type - totalArea,
                workingPlatformElevation, workingPlatformBearingCapacity, averageExistingGL,
                averageWTLevel - moved to diggs:RITreatedArea and diggs:WorkingPlatform, where each
                belongs to the thing it actually describes.

                THIS GROUP'S OWN extent IS NOT THE TREATED FOOTPRINT, and the two should not be
                assumed equal. Per the base type, extent is "the area over which this group acts, or
                within which its members lie" - and a system's members routinely include exploratory
                boreholes drilled outside the ground-improvement footprint to characterize it. The
                treated footprint is diggs:RITreatedArea's featureExtent; this extent encloses
                everything the group contains, and is normally the larger of the two.

                MEMBERSHIP IS DECLARED BY THE MEMBERS, not enumerated here. diggs:LoadTransferPlatform
                and diggs:RIColumn each cite this feature through the inherited
                diggs:samplingFeatureGroupRef; this feature holds no references to them. Before GR1,
                diggs:RIColumn carried an optional diggs:samplingFeatureRef for the same purpose,
                which conflated MEMBERSHIP with the CONTAINMENT sense that same element name carries
                on an installation - see diggs:samplingFeatureGroupRef's own documentation
                (core/Common.xsd) for the full distinction.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RigidInclusionSystemType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractSamplingFeatureGroupType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="whenConstructed"
                        type="diggs:TimeIntervalPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The date range spanning the exploration, design, construction and post-installation construction of the rigid inclusion system's columns and load transfer platforms.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="purpose" type="string"/>
                    <!-- The loadTransferPlatforms containment array was removed here. Load transfer
                         platforms are now top-level sampling features that cite this system through
                         the inherited, optional samplingFeatureGroupRef (GR1/GR2, 2026-08-22,
                         group-redesign - originally a mandatory samplingFeatureRef, retired with
                         this system's reclassification as a group); see LoadTransferPlatformType
                         below. Containment made a foundation system undeliverable until at least one
                         platform existed, which is backwards from construction order - the columns
                         are installed before the platform is built over them. -->
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="LoadTransferPlatform" type="diggs:LoadTransferPlatformType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractPlanarSamplingFeature">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A load transfer platform: the compacted granular layer, usually
                reinforced with geosynthetics, constructed over a field of rigid inclusions to
                distribute structural loads onto the column heads.

                A PLAIN PLANAR SAMPLING FEATURE, NOT AN INSTALLATION (GR1, 2026-08-22,
                group-redesign). Demoted from diggs:AbstractPlanarInstallation, which mandated
                samplingFeatureRef in the CONTAINMENT sense - "built within the referenced feature" -
                a sense that never fit an LTP well, since a platform is not constructed inside the
                rigid inclusion system, it is constructed over the columns as a distinct layer. It
                cites the system it belongs to through the inherited, optional
                diggs:samplingFeatureGroupRef instead - MEMBERSHIP, not containment. The
                installation-only properties the demotion drops
                (installationConstructionEvent, installationEquipment) were verified unused across
                the entire corpus; constructionMethod and constructionEquipment, declared below,
                already cover what installationEquipment would have.

                The rigid inclusions beneath a platform are not enumerated here: a column's
                membership is determined by testing its position against this platform's
                featureExtent.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="LoadTransferPlatformType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractPlanarSamplingFeatureType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="whenConstructed"
                        type="diggs:TimeIntervalPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Time during which the LTP was constructed.
</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="totalArea" type="diggs:AreaMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The total area of the LTP.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="constructionMethod"
                        type="diggs:SpecificationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Contains a Specification object that is used to describe the method of LTP construction.
</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="constructionEquipment"
                        type="diggs:EquipmentPropertyType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Time during which the LTP was constructed.
</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="materialType" type="diggs:LithologyPropertyType"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="platformType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The construction type of the load transfer platform, as
                                built - unreinforced granular fill, geogrid-reinforced fill,
                                soil-mixed / cemented raft, or (the extreme case) a structural
                                concrete slab (V2 Design row 50).

                                DISTINCT FROM materialType, WHICH STAYS A LITHOLOGY. materialType
                                classifies what the fill is made of geologically (a soil/rock
                                classification); it cannot say "structural concrete slab", which is
                                not a lithology at all. platformType is the construction-method
                                classification row 50 actually asks for. Named to match
                                diggs:RILTPSpecificationType/platformType, the design-side
                                counterpart.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="elevationTopLTP"
                        type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The elevation of the top of the load transfer platform.

                                One LTP is one zone, and a zone has one top elevation applying
                                across its whole extent - including where that extent is
                                geometrically discontinuous, a diggs:MultiSurface of several
                                disjoint polygons. Surfaces that did not share the elevation would
                                be separate LoadTransferPlatform features. So this is a definite
                                position, not an averaged level.

                                PRECEDENCE HAS TWO BRANCHES, as for
                                RigidInclusionSystemType/workingPlatformElevation. Where this
                                platform's featureExtent is 3-D or compound the geometry prevails;
                                where it is 2-D the geometry carries no elevation and THIS PROPERTY
                                IS THE PREVAILING VALUE. In practice LTP extents are commonly
                                delivered as 2-D plan polygons, so this property is usually the only
                                carrier.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="impliedLTPThickness" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The thickness of the load transfer platform, inferred from the LTP top and workding platform elevations.

                                Optional, matching its two source elevations: elevationTopLTP on this
                                type and workingPlatformElevation on RigidInclusionSystemType are both
                                [0..1], so a value inferred from them cannot itself be mandatory. Left
                                mandatory through C1, which fixed the equivalent problem for
                                loadTransferPlatforms/rigidInclusions containment but did not revisit
                                this element - found and corrected in review, 2026-08-16.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="geosynthetics"
                        type="diggs:GeosyntheticArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A property that holds the Geosyntheic objects installed within this LTP.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <!-- The rigidInclusions containment array was removed here. Rigid inclusions
                         are top-level sampling features; the set underlying this platform is
                         derived by testing each column's position against this platform's
                         featureExtent, not enumerated. No RI-to-LTP reference is declared either:
                         the columns precede the platform in construction time, so such a reference
                         would point forward and would require delivered column records to be
                         edited once the platform was built. -->
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RITreatedArea" type="diggs:RITreatedAreaType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractPlanarSamplingFeature">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The area of the site treated by a rigid inclusion system - the ground
                improvement footprint that includes the rigid columns and may extend beyond any associated foundation or embankment. Although its featureExtent inherits from AbstractPlanarSamplingFeature, and therefore may include any concrete surface geometry, it is typically expressed as a PlanarSurface in a 2D CRS, since the footprint's elevation is of no relevance.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RITreatedAreaType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Concrete type extending AbstractPlanarSamplingFeatureType.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractPlanarSamplingFeatureType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="totalArea" type="diggs:AreaMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The total area of the treated area - the area encompassing the installation of rigid inclusion columns</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="1" name="workingPlatform"
                        type="diggs:WorkingPlatformPropertyType"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="seismicSiteClass" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The seismic site classification (IBC/ASCE 7 Site Class
                                A-F) of the site after ground improvement, based on the composite
                                properties of the treated ground mass (V2 Post-installation row 31).

                                MOVED HERE FROM diggs:RigidInclusionSystemType (2026-08-26). A site
                                class is a property of the treated ground, and the system is a
                                GROUP - a bundling construct whose membership may include
                                exploratory features lying outside the treated area entirely, so a
                                ground property stated there had no well-defined subject. This is
                                the treated area, so this is where it belongs.

                                A CLASSIFICATION, NOT THE MEASUREMENT IT IS DERIVED FROM. The
                                composite shear-wave velocity a Site Class determination is based on
                                is a genuinely separate fact, already expressible today - a measured
                                outcome via diggs:LabVelocityTest or diggs:GeophysicalFieldSurvey, or
                                stated as a diggs:PerformanceCriterion where a minimum Vs is a design
                                requirement (see specialty/Grouting.xsd's Vs criterion precedent).
                                This element carries only the interpreted code that measurement
                                supports, kept distinct per row 31's own split verdict - conflating a
                                measured quantity with a derived classification under one value is
                                the same trap Design row 2 (governing standard vs. design method) was
                                corrected out of.

                                THE ACHIEVED CLASS, NOT THE INTENDED ONE. Its design-side
                                counterpart is diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType/seismicSiteClass, which
                                carries the class the design PREDICTS; where the two differ, that
                                divergence is the finding, and neither value should be edited to
                                match the other.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="WorkingPlatform" type="diggs:WorkingPlatformType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A surface prepared within the treated area before ground improvement begins that is designed to carry the load of equipment used in constructing the rigid inclusion columns and load transfer platform(s). The platform is prepared to be horizontal and, depending on site conditions, may be underlain by granular fill deposited over the natural ground surface in order to provide sufficient supportive bearing capacity. If only one working platform exists within the treated area, its wpExtent property may be omitted and its extent is assumed to be the same as RITreatedArea. Its wpElevation property is mandatory and overrides any elevation expressed in wpExtent in the event that wpExtent is expressed in a 3D CRS.

                A COMPONENT OBJECT, NOT A SAMPLING FEATURE. It extends diggs:AbstractObjectType and
                is reachable only through diggs:RITreatedAreaType/workingPlatform, because a working
                platform bounds the work rather than being a thing the work is performed on: nothing
                is sampled from it, no observation targets it, and it has no identity outside the
                treated area it was prepared within. This is the same call made for
                diggs:Geosynthetic, which is likewise a component of the
                diggs:LoadTransferPlatform that contains it.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="WorkingPlatformType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractObjectType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="wpExtent" type="diggs:PlanarSurfacePropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The surface extent of the working platform. If omitted, there can be only one WorkingPlatform and the extent is assumed to be the same as RITreatedArea. Typically expressed in a 2D CRS because the platform's elevation must be expressed in the mandatory wpElevation property, which overrides any elevation reported in wpExtent.

                                MAY BE STATED INLINE OR BY REFERENCE. diggs:PlanarSurfacePropertyType
                                carries gml:AssociationAttributeGroup and an optional content model,
                                so where a platform's extent is genuinely the same polygon as
                                something already declared - one of several platforms coinciding with
                                a diggs:LoadTransferPlatform's featureExtent, say - this may cite that
                                surface by xlink:href rather than restate it. Note the single-platform
                                case needs no reference at all: omit this property entirely and the
                                extent is the RITreatedArea's.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="totalArea" type="diggs:AreaMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The total area of the working platform. This is a convenience property designed so that consumers do not need to compute total area from RITreatmentArea/featureExtent or, if present, wpExstent. If there is disagreement between totalArea and the area computed from the geometry, the geometry prevails.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="1" name="wpElevation" type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The elevation of the working platform. As the platform is required to be horizontal, this value is a
                                position, not an averaged level, that should be expressed as a one-dimensional position in a vertical CRS. This property is mandatory - its value overrides any elevation that may be expressed in the working platform's wpExtent or in the RITreatedArea's featureExtent.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="wpMaterial" type="diggs:LithologyPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The material of the fill placed beneath the working
                                platform - typically a well-graded compacted granular fill.

                                DESCRIBES THE FILL, NOT THE PLATFORM, WHICH IS WHY IT IS OPTIONAL.
                                The working platform is a SURFACE, not a layer, so it has no
                                material of its own. Fill beneath it exists only where it is needed
                                to reach the bearing capacity the installation equipment requires;
                                where the natural ground already provides that, no fill is placed
                                and this property is simply absent.

                                PAIRS WITH avgFillThickness, and the two are absent together. That
                                property is the same fill's thickness, "assumed to be zero" when
                                omitted - the same no-fill case this property's absence records.
                                Between them they answer what was placed and how much of it; there
                                is no separate platform-thickness property, because a surface has
                                no thickness.

                                NOT THE SAME THING AS diggs:LoadTransferPlatformType/materialType,
                                though both are diggs:LithologyPropertyType and the two are easily
                                confused - this is F1's conflation in the V2 workbook, resolved.
                                A working platform is temporary construction access, prepared
                                BEFORE the columns are installed and characterized by the rig load
                                it must carry; this property describes the fill under it. A load
                                transfer platform is permanent structure, built AFTER the columns
                                over their finished heads, and characterized by how it distributes
                                load onto them; its materialType describes the platform itself,
                                which genuinely is a layer with a thickness. Where a site's LTP
                                happens to be founded on the former working platform, that is a
                                construction-sequence fact about two distinct features, not one
                                feature named twice.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="wpBearingCapacity" type="diggs:PressureMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The bearing capacity the working platform was verified to
                                provide before rigid inclusion installation began - sufficient to
                                carry the maximum ground pressure exerted by the drill rigs, grout
                                pumps and support equipment used to install the columns.

                                A THRESHOLD VERIFIED ONCE, NOT A DESIGN VALUE TRACKED AGAINST AN
                                AS-BUILT OUTCOME. Working platform adequacy is a go/no-go check made
                                before RI work starts: if the existing ground cannot carry the required equipment,
                                the site is modified, typically with a granular platform layer, often
                                geosynthetic-reinforced, until it can.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="avgFillThickness" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The average thickness of fill placed over the existing ground level below
                                the working platform surface. If not present, this value is assumed to be zero. The average natural ground surface elevation below the working platform may be computed from wpElevation - avgFillThickness.

                                A LENGTH, NOT A POSITION - the one property in this object that is
                                not an elevation. A thickness has magnitude but no location, it must
                                be able to take the value zero (which the documentation above relies
                                on), and it is subtracted FROM an elevation to yield one. Contrast
                                wpElevation and avgWTLevel beside it, which are both
                                diggs:PointLocationPropertyType because each names a definite place
                                in a vertical CRS.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="avgWTLevel"
                        type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The average elevation of the water table beneath the
                                working platform. A position, and therefore expressed as a 1D PointLocation defined by a vertical CRS.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <complexType name="WorkingPlatformPropertyType">
        <sequence minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
            <element ref="diggs:WorkingPlatform"/>
        </sequence>
    </complexType>
    <element name="Geosynthetic" type="diggs:GeosyntheticType"/>
    <complexType name="GeosyntheticType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractObjectType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="reinforcementType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Identifies the type of geosynthetic reinforcement used. Intended to come from a controlled list of terms and parallels diggs:GeosyntheticLayerDesignType/diggs:reinforcementType</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="heightAboveWorkingPlatform"
                        type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The measured height of the geosynthetic above the working platform.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="elevation" type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The elevation of the geosynthetic layer within the LTP.

                                An elevation is a position, not a length. Use srsName on the
                                PointLocation to state the vertical CRS, and populate uomLabels so
                                the unit is readable without resolving it. This is a convenience property, only necessary where extent is not reported in a 3D CRS.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="extent" type="diggs:PlanarSurfacePropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A planar surface (polygon) whose geospatial coordinates mark the lateral extent of the geosynthetic within the LTP. If this element is not present, it is assumeed that the geosynthetic extends across the entire LTP.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="direction" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>machine / cross-machine / isotropic - the as-installed
                                orientation. Added by S17 (Post-installation Parameter Analysis row
                                20, "layers, orientation, tension, overlap, cover" - confirmed gap,
                                `GeosyntheticType` carried none of the four). Named direction, not
                                orientation, to match diggs:GeosyntheticLayerDesignType/direction
                                (core/Common.xsd) - the design counterpart this value is checked
                                against.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="installedTension"
                        type="diggs:ForcePerLengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The tensile force per unit width measured or recorded at
                                installation. Compare
                                diggs:GeosyntheticLayerDesignType/designTension, the design
                                counterpart - that object is split by load path (arching, lateral
                                spreading, ...); this is the single as-installed value an ITP
                                records, with no load-path split to make.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="overlapLength" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The overlap between this panel and the adjacent
                                one.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="coverThickness" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The thickness of fill placed over this layer.
                                Distinct from heightAboveWorkingPlatform (the layer's own height
                                above the working platform) and elevation (the layer's absolute
                                level): this is the depth of material subsequently placed on top of
                                it, which neither existing element states.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <complexType name="GeosyntheticArrayPropertyType">
        <sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <element ref="diggs:Geosynthetic"/>
        </sequence>
    </complexType>
    <element name="Reinforcement" type="diggs:ReinforcementType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Steel reinforcement occupying one interval of a rigid inclusion column -
                a cage over the top few metres at an edge column, or full length where bending
                governs. Reinforcement in RIs is the exception rather than the rule; where present
                it is zoned, which is why this object carries the interval it occupies rather than
                a bare length.

                ONE OBJECT, BOTH SIDES OF THE PLAN/REALIZED BOUNDARY, per the Installation
                analysis's G5: the design states what cage is required, the as-built states what
                cage went in, and they are the same shape seen from two ends. diggs:RIColumnDesignType
                carries this as the required reinforcement; diggs:RIColumnType carries it as
                the installed reinforcement. Repeatable on either host, for a column with more than
                one reinforced zone.

                intervalLocation is modelled on specialty/DeepFoundation.xsd's TaperType -
                the in-schema precedent for a depth-interval-scoped property on a linear
                installation.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="ReinforcementType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractComponentObjectBaseType">
                <sequence>
                    <element name="intervalLocation" type="diggs:LinearExtentPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The interval of the column this reinforcement occupies -
                                from head, from toe, or full length - as a two-point linear extent
                                along the column, matching diggs:TaperType/intervalLocation.
                                Mandatory: a reinforcement zone reported without its extent is not
                                interpretable.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="reinforcementType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The configuration of the reinforcement - rebar cage,
                                single central bar, structural steel section, and so on.
                                Distinct from steelGrade and barSize below, which describe the
                                material and the individual bars rather than the
                                arrangement.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="reinforcementPurpose" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Why reinforcement is present over this interval - tension
                                zone, bending, seismic, ASIRI Domain 1, and so on. The Design
                                analysis singles this out as what a reviewer actually checks, ahead
                                of the ratios and areas below.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="steelRatio" type="diggs:AreaPerAreaMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Rho_s: the ratio of steel cross-sectional area to gross
                                column cross-sectional area over this interval.
                                diggs:AreaPerAreaMeasureType, not DimensionlessMeasureType, for the
                                same reason as diggs:RIColumnDesignType/areaReplacementRatio - an
                                area-per-area quantity class forces an explicit uom that keeps a
                                fraction from being misread as a percentage or vice
                                versa.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="steelArea" type="diggs:AreaMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A_s: the total cross-sectional area of steel over this
                                interval.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="barSize" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The bar designation (for example US #8, or metric 25M).
                                A coded designation rather than a bare diameter, since bar-numbering
                                conventions differ by standard and a diameter alone loses which
                                convention produced it.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="barCount" type="positiveInteger">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The number of bars in the cage over this
                                interval.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="steelGrade" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The steel grade or strength class (for example Grade 60,
                                B500B).</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <complexType name="ReinforcementArrayPropertyType">
        <sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
            <element ref="diggs:Reinforcement"/>
        </sequence>
    </complexType>
    <!-- CMCSubmittedDesign was declared here, typed CMCArrayType. Both are retired (C4). The
         array scope form they served - a coverage whose range carried a planned-identifier column
         plus per-column properties - was retracted because a coverage row has no gml:id and so
         cannot be referenced by a ProgressEvent, an AcceptanceCriterion or a TargetSample. The
         concept is now RIColumnDesign plus, where columns vary arbitrarily, one
         TargetSamplingFeature per column. -->
    <element name="RIColumn" type="diggs:RIColumnType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractLinearSamplingFeature">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A sampling feature describing the location and properties of a single rigid inclusion element (CMC).</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RIColumnType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractLinearSamplingFeatureType">
                <sequence>
                    <element name="elementClass" type="gml:CodeWithAuthorityType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The role this column plays in the works, as distinct from
                                what it is made of or how it is built - trial, test, production, or
                                sacrificial (elementClass.xml). Mandatory, and codeSpace is mandatory
                                on it (inherited from gml:CodeWithAuthorityType): the distinction
                                governs which acceptance criteria apply and how a test result may be
                                extrapolated, so it must be stated rather than left to be inferred.

                                THE AUTHORITATIVE SIGNAL, SUPERSEDING samplingFeatureGroupRef's
                                ABSENCE (inherited from diggs:AbstractSamplingFeatureType; GR1,
                                2026-08-22, group-redesign). Before EC1 (2026-08-19), a trial column
                                was identified only by omitting the element then named
                                samplingFeatureRef - a real but indirect signal, easy to mistake for
                                an oversight rather than a deliberate statement. GR1 removed that
                                element outright: what was optional samplingFeatureRef on RIColumn is
                                now the inherited, equally optional samplingFeatureGroupRef, and
                                identification remains exclusively via THIS element regardless. A
                                trial column may still be left out of any group, or may be included
                                in one where its data informs other columns' interpretation - either
                                is a legitimate use of the group reference and neither reopens the
                                old absence-based convention this element replaced.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A description of the type of column, intended to come from a controlled list of terms, often associated with the construction method or type of equipment used to construct the column.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="whenConstructed"
                        type="diggs:TimeIntervalPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The period of time when the hole is constructed. This
                                should include the entire time the drill rig is on
                                site.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="constructionMethod"
                        type="diggs:RIConstructionMethodPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Information regarding construction of the rigid inclusion over
                                different depth and/or time 
                               intervals</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="constructionEvent"
                        type="diggs:RIEventPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Information about events recorded during construction of
                                the rigid inclusion.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="groundSurfaceElevation"
                        type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The elevation of the ground surface, or of the working
                                platform where one is present, at this column.

                                An elevation is a position, not a length, and here that is
                                load-bearing rather than cosmetic. A rigid inclusion conflates the
                                drilled hole and the grout column into a single sampling feature,
                                and the column takes precedence for geometry: the centerLine runs
                                from the column head to the column tip, not from ground level to the
                                tip. Depths recorded during drilling - measurement-while-drilling
                                data, and RIEventType/riDepth - are measured instead from the ground
                                or platform datum the rig works from. This property is what relates
                                the two, and without it drilling-time depths cannot be placed on the
                                column.

                                Because it is a position it may be stated in whichever frame it was
                                actually measured: as a one-dimensional position in a vertical CRS,
                                as a one-dimensional position in this column's own linear
                                referencing system, or as a full three-dimensional position. A
                                scalar could only ever express one of those. Populate uomLabels so
                                the unit is readable without resolving the CRS.

                                It corresponds to the property of the same name on
                                diggs:AbstractPileType, which carries it even though piles are
                                installations that could in principle derive it from a parent
                                borehole feature.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="cutoffElevation"
                        type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Elevation of the column cutoff - the top of the finished
                                column.

                                An elevation is a position, not a length; see
                                groundSurfaceElevation for the frame options and the uomLabels
                                guidance. Optional and informational, carried for symmetry with
                                columnLength. Unlike groundSurfaceElevation this value IS derivable
                                from the geometry, being the first point of the centerLine, so WHERE
                                THE TWO DISAGREE THE GEOMETRY PREVAILS.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnLength" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnDiameter" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="theoreticalGroutVolume"
                        type="diggs:VolumeMeasureType"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="placedGroutVolume" type="diggs:VolumeMeasureType"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="cutoffMethod"
                        type="diggs:SpecificationPropertyType"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnCapheadPresent" type="boolean"/>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="meetsVerticality" type="boolean">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>True when verticality of the rigid inclusion meets specified verticality tolerances. Such tolerances should be specified in the ColumnSpec object of RIDesignSpec.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="waterStrike"
                        type="diggs:WaterStrikePropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Information on when ground water is struck during
                                drilling of the hole.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="reinforcement"
                        type="diggs:ReinforcementArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The steel reinforcement actually installed in this
                                column, by zone. Named to match
                                diggs:RIColumnDesignType/reinforcement, the design counterpart, per
                                the same naming convention as columnDiameter and columnLength.
                                Absent where the column is unreinforced, the common
                                case.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="TargetRIColumn" type="diggs:TargetRIColumnType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractTargetObject">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A specialization of diggs:TargetSamplingFeature for a planned rigid
                inclusion column, carrying the one property an RI column plan needs beyond the
                base: its role in the works (EC1, 2026-08-19). Where a planned column needs
                nothing beyond diggs:TargetSamplingFeatureType's own fields, plain
                diggs:TargetSamplingFeature is still used directly, per that type's own
                documentation - diggs:RIColumn is one of the realized heads it already names.
                TargetRIColumn exists for the case a plan must also commit to the column's role
                (trial, test, production, sacrificial) ahead of construction, the same reason
                diggs:TargetBorehole and diggs:TargetInstallation (core/Core.xsd) exist for their
                own feature kinds. Substitutes diggs:AbstractTargetObject directly rather than
                diggs:TargetSamplingFeature - concrete DIGGS elements are never substitution-group
                heads - but its type extends diggs:TargetSamplingFeatureType, the same pattern
                diggs:TargetBoreholeType uses.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="TargetRIColumnType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:TargetSamplingFeatureType">
                <sequence>
                    <element name="elementClass" type="gml:CodeWithAuthorityType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Matches diggs:RIColumnType/elementClass in name, type,
                                dictionary (elementClass.xml) and mandatory cardinality - the
                                planned counterpart of the realized column's own role statement,
                                committed to at design time rather than left to be inferred once
                                the column exists. A planned trial column states elementClass=trial
                                here for the same reason its eventual as-built diggs:RIColumn does;
                                nothing about the trial/test/production/sacrificial distinction is
                                specific to either side of the plan/realized
                                boundary.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <!-- RIArrayPropertyType was removed with the rigidInclusions containment array that was its
         only use. LTPArrayPropertyType was removed with loadTransferPlatforms for the same reason.
         Both are gone rather than left unreferenced: neither had any other use anywhere in the
         schema. -->
    <complexType name="RIConstructionMethodType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The construction-method SUMMARY carried on the feature - one or more
                intervals or discrete method changes over the depth or time of construction,
                referenced from diggs:RIColumnType/constructionMethod.

                THIS STAYS THE SUMMARY. THE REAL-TIME RECORD LIVES ON THE ACTIVITY. C3 added
                diggs:RIConstructionActivity, whose installationRecord property carries the
                continuous depth- or time-indexed telemetry stream this type only summarizes in a
                handful of intervals. WHERE THE TWO DISAGREE, THE RECORD ON THE ACTIVITY GOVERNS -
                this object is authored or derived from that stream, not the other way round, so a
                conflict means this summary is stale or was rounded, not that the detailed record
                is wrong.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractNamedRoleObjectType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" ref="diggs:location">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The location within the sampling feature that corresponds
                                to the particular construction method being referenced here. If
                                omitted, it is assumed that the procedures specified apply to the
                                entire extent of the sampling feature</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="methodTime"
                        type="diggs:TimeIntervalPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The period of time during which the construction event is
                                occurring</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="holeStability" type="string">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Uncontrolled string describing stability conditions of
                                the hole during this interval</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="constructionMethod"
                        type="diggs:SpecificationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Contains a Specification object that may be used to
                                describe any details regarding the method of construction. It should
                                not be used to simply provide a name for the construction method.
                                That value should be entered in the gml:name property for this
                                object.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="constructionEquipment"
                        type="diggs:EquipmentPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A property element that contains an Equipment feature (or
                                reference to one) that details the construction equipment
                                used.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="cuttingToolInfo"
                        type="diggs:CuttingToolArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The cutting tool info property.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="drillPipeInfo"
                        type="diggs:DrillPipeArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The drill pipe info property.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="hydraulicFluidFlowRate"
                        type="diggs:VolumePerTimeMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The flow rate of the hydraulic fluid.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" ref="diggs:groutRef"/>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RIConstructionMethod" type="diggs:RIConstructionMethodType"/>
    <complexType name="RIConstructionMethodPropertyType">
        <sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
            <element ref="diggs:RIConstructionMethod"/>
        </sequence>
        <attributeGroup ref="gml:OwnershipAttributeGroup"/>
        <attributeGroup ref="gml:AssociationAttributeGroup"/>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RIEvent" type="diggs:RIEventType"/>
    <complexType name="RIEventType">
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractEventType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="riBottom" type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Measured Depth to the base of the rigid inclusion at this event time,
                                reported in the RI's linear referencing system.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <choice>
                        <element minOccurs="0" name="depthToWater"
                            type="diggs:PointLocationPropertyType">
                            <annotation>
                                <documentation>Depth to Water Surface as measured at this event
                                    time, recorded in the hole's linear referencing
                                    system.</documentation>
                            </annotation>
                        </element>
                        <element fixed="true" minOccurs="0" name="isDry" type="boolean">
                            <annotation>
                                <documentation>Indicates whether dry.</documentation>
                            </annotation>
                        </element>
                    </choice>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <complexType name="RIEventPropertyType">
        <sequence minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="1">
            <element ref="diggs:RIEvent"/>
        </sequence>
        <attributeGroup ref="gml:OwnershipAttributeGroup"/>
        <attributeGroup ref="gml:AssociationAttributeGroup"/>
    </complexType>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- RI PROGRAM MODULES                                           -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Reinstated in C4 against the program-module heads introduced in B3. What was here before
         was a commented sketch whose design/specification elements were untyped - <element
         name="riConcept"/> defaults to anyType and admits anything - which is why it was never
         released.

         RETIRED here, deliberately, and not to be reinstated:
           CMCArrayType, CMCArrayPropertyType, and the children columnIds, columnTopLocations,
             columnLengths - the array scope form; see the note above RIColumn.
           RITreatedAreaDesignPropertyType - modules now hang off the program's generic design slot.
           CMCConstructionActivity / CMCConstructionActivityType - the RI construction activity is
             built properly in C3, with its measurement-while-drilling coverage. The sketch carried
             torqueApplied, penetrationPerRevolution, penetrationRate, withdrawalRate,
             rotationSpeeed (sic), groutPressure and groutFlowRate; that list is preserved in
             docs/build/C4/NOTES.md as the only record of what the original authors expected to
             record, and C3 decides which are activity properties and which are coverage columns.
           RigidInclusionProgramType/explorationPhaseDesign, riPerformanceSpec, riFeasibility,
             riConcept, riDesignSpec - each named a KIND of module. The kind is now carried by the
             module object's own type, not by the slot it sits in.
           RITreatedAreaDesignType/columnDesign, ltpDesign - these become RIColumnDesign and RILTPDesign
             (C2), declared as siblings under the program and citing RITreatedAreaDesign through
             basedOnRef rather than being contained by it.
           RITreatedAreaDesignType/predictedPerformance, globalStability - untyped sketches of design
             outputs. Retired rather than guessed at; if they return they need real types. -->
    <element name="RigidInclusionProgram" type="diggs:RigidInclusionProgramType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractProgram">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A program of rigid inclusion ground improvement work: the designs the
                program issues, the specifications it must satisfy, and the bases those were
                prepared from.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RigidInclusionProgramType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for a rigid inclusion program. Carries three generic module slots
                rather than one slot per kind of design or specification: what kind of module an
                object is, is carried by the object's own type and by which substitution group it
                belongs to, so a slot per kind would restate it and go stale as kinds are
                added.

                DOCUMENT ORDER IS design*, THEN specification*, THEN basis*, STRICTLY GROUPED - not
                interleaved. This is an ordinary XSD sequence constraint, not a logical one: a
                design's programSpecificationRef may point at a specification declared later in the
                document (the antecedent rule governs logical dependency, not document position), but
                every design element must precede every specification element regardless of which
                design cites which specification, and every specification must precede every basis
                element the same way. A hand-authored instance grouping objects by narrative instead
                - a TargetMeasurement next to its own criterion, say - will fail here. Found building
                the B8 worked fragment; recorded on the type itself so it is not rediscovered the same
                way twice.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractProgramType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="design"
                        type="diggs:ProgramDesignPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A design this program issues - what is intended to be
                                built. Holds any member of the diggs:AbstractProgramDesign
                                substitution group, so an RI program may carry an RITreatedAreaDesign, an
                                RIColumnDesign and a DesignGroutMix declared in Grouting.xsd
                                alongside one another.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="specification"
                        type="diggs:ProgramSpecificationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A specification this program must satisfy - the
                                constraints and tolerances the work is judged against. Holds any
                                member of the diggs:AbstractProgramSpecification substitution
                                group.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="basis"
                        type="diggs:ProgramBasisPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A basis the program's designs were prepared from - the
                                ground model, requirement set or other evidence relied on. Holds any
                                member of the diggs:AbstractProgramBasis substitution
                                group.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RITreatedAreaDesign" type="diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractProgramDesign">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The site-wide design for a rigid inclusion foundation system: the design
                properties that hold across the whole system, from which zone- and element-level
                designs depart.

                It is a thin carrier by intention. It does not CONTAIN the column, LTP or grout
                designs - those are declared as siblings under the program. An RIColumnDesign or
                RILTPDesign for a particular zone cites this object through basedOnRef, and property
                resolution then runs most-specific-first: a zone design that states no value for a
                property inherits the value stated here. Containment was rejected for the same
                reason it was rejected for the RI sampling features - a contained design cannot be
                delivered before its parent exists, cannot be cited from outside without walking
                into its parent, and a grout mix used across several zones would have to be
                duplicated.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RITreatedAreaDesignType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the site-wide rigid inclusion system design. Inherits scope,
                basedOnRef, programSpecificationRef and basisRef from
                diggs:AbstractProgramDesignType.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractProgramDesignType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="groutDesignRef"
                        type="diggs:FeatureReferenceType">
                        <annotation>
                            <appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement">diggs:DesignGroutMix</appinfo>
                            <documentation>The grout mix design or designs adopted site-wide. A
                                reference rather than a contained object: a mix design is a
                                COMPONENT of the work, not a generalization of it, so basedOnRef
                                would be the wrong relation, and the same mix is normally used
                                across several zones. The referenced DesignGroutMix is declared as a
                                sibling under the program's design slot, and precedes this object -
                                the mix is designed before the system design adopts it.

                                A zone-level design may override this by stating its own
                                groutDesignRef; per basedOnRef resolution the most specific value
                                wins.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="action"
                        type="diggs:DesignActionArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The applied loads this system design must resist -
                                embankment self-weight, traffic, a structure or slab load, a
                                surcharge, and so on (Design Parameter Analysis rows 78-79). Placed
                                here rather than on diggs:RILTPDesignType because not every action a
                                site must resist is transmitted through a load transfer platform - a
                                footing or tank load may bear on RI-improved ground directly - and
                                because a single site-wide design is exactly where
                                diggs:DesignAction's own appliesToZoneRef earns its place: each
                                repetition can govern a different part of the site under one
                                RITreatedAreaDesign, the same way groutDesignRef above may repeat for
                                different mixes used across zones.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="verification"
                        type="diggs:DesignVerificationArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Site-wide design checks - global/slope stability,
                                lateral spreading, seismic/liquefaction (Design Parameter Analysis
                                rows 84, 86, 88). Placed here rather than on
                                diggs:RIColumnDesignType because these three are checks against the
                                whole foundation system's stability and behaviour, not against one
                                column - contrast diggs:RIColumnDesignType/verification, which
                                carries the column-level structural checks (buckling, shear) from
                                the same analysis's Group C.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="settlementEfficacy"
                        type="diggs:DimensionlessMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>E_tass: settlement reduction of the treated ground versus
                                untreated ground. Row 81 (S20). A site-wide serviceability
                                comparison, not derived from the LTP's stress pair the way
                                diggs:RILTPDesignType/loadEfficacy is - contrast that
                                element.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="settlementEfficacyDefinition" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Which definition settlementEfficacy uses. See
                                diggs:RILTPDesignType/loadEfficacyDefinition for why this
                                accompanies the value rather than being assumed.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="predictedSettlement"
                        type="diggs:PredictedSettlementPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The predicted settlement of the system, broken down by
                                component (Design Parameter Analysis row 89). Repeats per
                                component - see diggs:PredictedSettlement's own documentation. One
                                repeated wrapper element per component, matching
                                diggs:RIProgramBasisType/factor's convention rather than an array
                                wrapper.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="differentialSettlement"
                        type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Delta-s: the predicted differential or surface
                                settlement. Row 90 (S20). MEANINGLESS WITHOUT ITS BASIS - "between
                                adjacent column heads? Across a unit cell? Over a 100 ft span? As an
                                angular distortion?" - see differentialSettlementBasis/
                                differentialSettlementBaseLength below, which state it. Modelled
                                with the same field set as
                                diggs:AbstractAcceptanceCriterionType's basis/baseLength (F3) rather
                                than a new wrapper object, so a predicted value and the
                                serviceability criterion it is checked against (row 91) are directly
                                comparable field by field.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="differentialSettlementBasis" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The unit of work differentialSettlement is assessed
                                over - per element, per unit cell, per 100 ft, angular distortion,
                                and so on. See differentialSettlement.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="differentialSettlementBaseLength"
                        type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The reference length differentialSettlement is assessed
                                over, where differentialSettlementBasis expresses a gradient (for
                                example "0.5 in per 100 ft") - the workbook's own example gives both
                                a magnitude form and a gradient form, different quantity classes.
                                See differentialSettlement.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="methodApplicabilityRatio"
                        type="diggs:AuthorizedParameterPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A dimensionless geometry or stiffness ratio that gates
                                whether an arching method's unit-cell idealisation is valid for this
                                zone's layout - h/(s-d), d/s, sx/sy and the EBGEO stiffness ratio
                                ks,T/ks today (Design Parameter Analysis row 13's "method-applicability
                                gate and geometry envelope"), open to whatever a future method's own
                                applicability check needs, dictionary-bound rather than a fixed set of
                                named elements for exactly that reason. Each repetition is a
                                diggs:AuthorizedParameter or diggs:ProvenancedParameter bound to a
                                definition in ri_design_properties.xml - diggs:AuthorizedParameterPropertyType,
                                not diggs:ParameterPropertyType, so the schema itself enforces a
                                dictionary-bound, mandatory-codeSpace name rather than merely
                                documenting the expectation.

                                PLACED HERE, NOT ON diggs:RIColumnDesignType OR
                                diggs:RILTPDesignType, FOR THE SAME REASON diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType/
                                verification IS: THIS IS A WHOLE-SYSTEM JUDGMENT, NOT A PROPERTY OF
                                ONE COMPONENT DESIGN. "Does this method's assumption hold for this
                                zone's layout" is the same kind of check as "is this zone globally
                                stable" - evaluated at the zone/system level even though its
                                ingredients (spacing, diameter on RIColumnDesignType;
                                embankmentHeight on RILTPDesignType) come from component designs.
                                h/(s-d) specifically needs both: it has no single home on either
                                component design, which is what forced the question this element
                                answers.

                                A SPECIFICALLY NAMED CONTAINER, NOT diggs:otherProperty, DELIBERATELY.
                                A property varying too freely to justify a fixed schema element still
                                benefits from a self-documenting slot rather than the generic escape
                                hatch: the dictionary's Occurrence sourceElementXpath/
                                conditionalElementXpath can then scope an entry to exactly this
                                element (ancestor::diggs:methodApplicabilityRatio) rather than to any
                                otherProperty on any design type, which both documents intent to a
                                schema browser and makes a source-system mapping unambiguous about
                                where a computed ratio belongs.

                                THE STIFFNESS GATE'S THRESHOLD IS NOT HERE. ks,T/ks itself (the
                                ratio) belongs in this element; the pass/fail requirement it must
                                clear is a method-applicability verdict, not a value, and belongs on a
                                diggs:DesignConformanceCriterion naming this property once that
                                machinery is extended to cover it - unbuilt, not addressed
                                here.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="seismicSiteClass" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The seismic site classification (IBC/ASCE 7 Site Class
                                A-F) the design PREDICTS for the treated ground after improvement.

                                A PREDICTED OUTPUT, WHICH IS WHY IT SITS BESIDE predictedSettlement
                                RATHER THAN IN A CRITERION. A design states what it expects the
                                improved ground to achieve; that is a design output of the same kind
                                as a predicted settlement, and it belongs on the design object. A
                                REQUIRED minimum site class is a different statement entirely - a
                                constraint the work is judged against - and belongs on a
                                diggs:PerformanceCriterion, following the precedent
                                specialty/Grouting.xsd already sets for a minimum shear-wave
                                velocity. Do not use this element to state a requirement: a
                                prediction that is missed is a finding, whereas a requirement that
                                is missed is a non-conformance, and collapsing the two loses exactly
                                that distinction.

                                ITS AS-BUILT COUNTERPART IS diggs:RITreatedAreaType/seismicSiteClass,
                                which carries the class actually achieved. The pair is intended to be
                                comparable: same vocabulary, same subject, one predicted and one
                                verified.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- C2 — RI DESIGN AND SPECIFICATION MODULES                     -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Four modules: two designs (what is intended to be built) and two
         specifications (what the work is judged against). All four are SIBLINGS under
         RigidInclusionProgramType's generic design/specification slots, never contained
         in one another - see RITreatedAreaDesignType above for why containment was rejected.

         ONE TYPE PER THING DESIGNED, NOT ONE TYPE PER SCOPE FORM. RIColumnDesign covers
         the site-wide, zone and single-element cases alike, because the scope is carried
         declaratively by the inherited ProgramScope (zoneExtent, plannedPositions or
         appliesToFeatureType) rather than by which type was chosen.

         EVERY PROPERTY IS OPTIONAL, AND THAT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN PERMISSIVE.
         Resolution runs per property up the basedOnRef chain, most-specific-first, so a
         zone design that states nothing but a length is a complete and meaningful object:
         it inherits diameter, grid and material from the design it specializes. A
         mandatory property would break that by forcing every specialization to restate
         a value it means to inherit.

         WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Limits and tolerances are NOT here. Position
         tolerance, verticality tolerance, minimum bearing-stratum embedment and the
         serviceability limits are each a diggs:DesignConformanceCriterion or
         diggs:PerformanceCriterion (B5), declared as a sibling under the program's
         specification slot and citing the specification below through basedOnRef - which
         is legal because AbstractAcceptanceCriterion substitutes AbstractProgramSpecification,
         so criterion-to-specification is a within-head chain. Building typed tolerance
         elements here as well would give two ways to state one limit with nothing
         correlating them: the exact failure mode that caused criterionKind to be split
         out of B5 rather than kept as a field.

         Per-soil-layer quantities (shaft and tip load-transfer moduli, negative skin
         friction, neutral-plane depth, toe and shaft resistances), reinforcement,
         geosynthetic layer design, design verifications and design soil parameters are
         all absent too. Each needs an object keyed by layer, load case or check - not a
         scalar element - and several of those objects are generic rather than RI-specific.
         They are later work, not omissions. -->
    <element name="RIColumnDesign" type="diggs:RIColumnDesignType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractProgramDesign">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The design of the rigid inclusion columns themselves: their layout,
                geometry, material strength and axial capacity, for whatever population the
                inherited scope declares.

                One object may govern three thousand columns or one. A site-wide design scopes
                itself with appliesToFeatureType; a zone design adds a zoneExtent polygon and
                usually a plannedPositions grid; a local exception carries a clipped polygon, no
                layout at all, and a basedOnRef to the design it departs from - inheriting the
                grid it does not restate.

                PROPERTIES THAT VARY WITHIN THE POPULATION DO NOT BELONG HERE. This object
                carries the uniform values - every column in this design is 400 mm. Where a
                particular column differs, that column is planned individually as a
                diggs:TargetSamplingFeature, which is a feature and so can carry its own typed
                properties, its own ProvenancedParameters, a gml:id that a criterion or a
                progress event can reference, and provenance for where its value came from. A
                coverage row could carry none of those, which is why the array design form was
                retracted.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RIColumnDesignType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the rigid inclusion column design. Inherits scope,
                basedOnRef, programSpecificationRef and basisRef from
                diggs:AbstractProgramDesignType.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractProgramDesignType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="gridPattern" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The plan arrangement of the columns - square,
                                rectangular, triangular, staggered or irregular. gml:CodeType with
                                a dictionary vocabulary rather than an enumeration, since layout
                                patterns are named differently across practices and the list is not
                                closed.

                                THE GEOMETRY PREVAILS where the inherited scope states a
                                plannedPositions grid: that grid's offsetVectors define the lattice
                                exactly, including patterns like an equilateral triangular
                                arrangement whose vectors are not orthogonal, and this code merely
                                names what they already express. Where scope carries no grid - a
                                zone-scoped or type-scoped design - THIS PROPERTY AND THE SPACINGS
                                BELOW ARE THE PREVAILING STATEMENT of the layout. A consumer cannot
                                tell which branch applies without inspecting the
                                scope.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="spacingX" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Centre-to-centre spacing of the columns along the first
                                grid axis.

                                STATE THIS ELEMENT ALONE for a square or triangular pattern, where
                                one spacing plus gridPattern is the whole layout. State spacingY as
                                well only for a rectangular pattern, where the two differ. Two
                                elements rather than one is deliberate: a single spacing element
                                would force a rectangular grid to encode two numbers under a
                                convention, and conventions in a data standard are read wrongly
                                sooner or later.

                                Informational where the scope carries a plannedPositions grid; see
                                gridPattern for both branches of the precedence
                                rule.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="spacingY" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Centre-to-centre spacing of the columns along the second
                                grid axis, for a rectangular pattern where it differs from spacingX.
                                Omit for square and triangular patterns. See spacingX.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="equivalentDiameter" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>d_Ers (EBGEO) / D_e (FHWA): the diameter of the circular
                                unit cell of equal area to the tributary area one inclusion supports,
                                derived from spacingX/spacingY and gridPattern. Reported explicitly
                                because the unit-cell radius, not the raw centre-to-centre spacing, is
                                what enters most arching and settlement formulations.

                                A TYPED CONVENIENCE ELEMENT, NOT A DICTIONARY PARAMETER, matching
                                totalArea/cutoffElevation elsewhere in this schema: it is
                                method-agnostic (used across arching AND settlement formulations
                                generically, not owned by one method) and computed entirely from
                                fields already on this type, so it earns the same typed-convenience
                                treatment rather than the dictionary escape hatch. Where this value
                                and a recomputation from spacingX/spacingY/gridPattern disagree, the
                                recomputation governs, per the general precedence rule (geometry/
                                detailed inputs over a stated convenience summary).</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="pileGroupLateralExtent"
                        type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>L_p: the outward extent the column group must cover
                                beyond the embankment crest. Row 87 (S20); pairs with
                                RILTPDesignType/embankmentHeight (row 76) per the Design Parameter
                                Analysis's own cross-reference.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnDiameter" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The nominal designed shaft diameter of the columns. Named
                                to match diggs:RIColumnType/columnDiameter, the as-built
                                counterpart, so that the design value and the constructed value
                                carry one name across the plan/realized boundary and a conformance
                                check has an unambiguous pair to compare.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnLength" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The designed column length, measured cut-off to toe.
                                Named to match diggs:RIColumnType/columnLength.

                                A LENGTH AND A TOE ELEVATION ARE DIFFERENT DATA, not two ways of
                                writing one number, which is why toeElevation below is a separate
                                element rather than an alternative encoding of this one. Designers
                                control whichever the ground dictates - commonly the toe elevation,
                                driven by penetration into the bearing stratum, with the length
                                falling out of it - and a file that carried only the derived one
                                would lose which was actually specified. State whichever governs,
                                or both.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="toeElevation" type="diggs:ElevationType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The designed elevation of the column toe.

                                A SCALAR diggs:ElevationType, NOT a position, and the distinction is
                                the one A6 drew across the whole schema: a position belongs to a
                                point on a feature, a level belongs to an area. This is a design
                                level adopted across whatever population the scope declares -
                                thousands of columns share it - so it has no definite point to be
                                the position of. Contrast diggs:RIColumnType/cutoffElevation
                                on the as-built side, which is a position because it belongs to one
                                built column and is extractable from that column's
                                centerLine.

                                The natural companion to the design bearing-stratum level: the toe
                                elevation is normally set by required penetration into that stratum,
                                and the required penetration itself is an acceptance criterion, not
                                a value here.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="areaReplacementRatio"
                        type="diggs:AreaPerAreaMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The ratio of inclusion (or cap) cross-sectional area to
                                the tributary area of one inclusion.

                                diggs:AreaPerAreaMeasureType, DELIBERATELY NOT DimensionlessMeasureType,
                                and this is the one unit choice in this type that is worth arguing
                                about. The quantity is quoted as a fraction about as often as a
                                percentage, and it is among the most-used numbers in rigid inclusion
                                design - coverage is typically under 10 percent beneath embankments,
                                an order of magnitude below conventional piled embankments. A file
                                that reports 0.05 where a reader expects 5 is a silent error with
                                consequences, and an area-per-area quantity class forces an explicit
                                uom that makes the mistake impossible to make quietly.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="designCompressiveStrength"
                        type="diggs:PressureMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The factored design compressive strength of the column
                                material - f_cd in Eurocode terms, the characteristic strength
                                reduced by a material factor, or the equivalent construction-method
                                and inspection-level reduction other practices apply.

                                Distinct from allowableMeanCompressiveStress below, and both may
                                bind. The CHARACTERISTIC strength this is derived from is a property
                                of the material, not of the column design, and belongs on the
                                referenced diggs:DesignGroutMix.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="allowableMeanCompressiveStress"
                        type="diggs:PressureMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>An absolute ceiling on mean compressive stress in the
                                column, applied on top of designCompressiveStrength rather than
                                instead of it.

                                THE TWO LIMITS BIND INDEPENDENTLY and a design governed by the
                                absolute cap looks different from one governed by the factored
                                strength; conflating them would lose which one controlled. Named
                                generically rather than after any one practice's symbol, because
                                several practices reach the same constraint by different routes -
                                a fixed cap in European ground-improvement recommendations, and
                                allowable-stress tables for deep foundation materials in US
                                building codes.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnAxialCapacity" type="diggs:ForceMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The structural axial resistance of one column - the
                                capacity of the column as a structural member, governed by its
                                material and cross-section.

                                Not to be confused with the GEOTECHNICAL capacity, which is the sum
                                of toe and shaft resistances and is a per-soil-layer computation.
                                Those quantities need an object keyed by geological unit and are not
                                yet built.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="designAxialLoad" type="diggs:ForceMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The axial load one column is designed to carry.

                                CARRIED AS A SINGLE VALUE HERE, AND THAT IS AN ACKNOWLEDGED
                                SIMPLIFICATION. The quantity genuinely varies by column position -
                                edge columns see very different demand from interior ones - and by
                                load combination, and practice does not even agree on which load to
                                use, ranging from allowable bearing pressure times area, through
                                sustained service loads, to a conservative assumption that the
                                columns take the entire applied load. Expressing that properly needs
                                a repeating object keyed by load case and carrying the basis of the
                                load, which is generic design-action machinery rather than anything
                                RI-specific, and is not yet built. Until it is, state the governing
                                value here, and carry the load case and basis as otherProperty on
                                the planned column.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="groutDesignRef"
                        type="diggs:FeatureReferenceType">
                        <annotation>
                            <appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement">diggs:DesignGroutMix</appinfo>
                            <documentation>The grout or concrete mix design adopted for these
                                columns. Overrides the site-wide groutDesignRef on any
                                diggs:RITreatedAreaDesign this design is based on; per basedOnRef
                                resolution the most specific value wins, and a design that states
                                none inherits the system's.

                                A reference rather than a contained object, and a plain reference
                                rather than basedOnRef, for the reason given on
                                diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType/groutDesignRef: a mix design is a COMPONENT
                                of the work, not a generalization of it, so inheritance is the wrong
                                relation, and one mix is normally shared across
                                zones.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="reinforcement"
                        type="diggs:ReinforcementArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The steel reinforcement required in this design, by
                                zone. Named to match diggs:RIColumnType/reinforcement, the
                                as-built counterpart. Absent where the design is unreinforced, the
                                common case - see diggs:ReinforcementType's own
                                documentation.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="verification"
                        type="diggs:DesignVerificationArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Column-level structural checks - buckling, shear
                                (Design Parameter Analysis rows 32, 33). Contrast
                                diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType/verification, which carries the site-wide
                                stability checks from the same analysis's Group I.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="loadTransferRelation"
                        type="diggs:LoadTransferRelationArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The shaft (t-z) and tip (q-z) load-transfer relations
                                adopted for this design, one per geotechnical unit the columns pass
                                through or bear on (Design Parameter Analysis rows 37, 38; supports
                                39 and 46). Repeats freely: a column typically passes through
                                several units, each with its own relation per mechanism, so a
                                multi-layer site states several diggs:LoadTransferRelation entries
                                here, not one.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="geoUnitDesignQuantity"
                        type="diggs:GeoUnitDesignQuantityArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The remaining per-soil-layer design outputs - negative
                                skin friction limit, neutral-plane depth and peak axial force, toe
                                and shaft resistance, limit unit tip resistance, the adopted
                                bearing-stratum level, and subgrade reaction modulus (Design
                                Parameter Analysis rows 39-44, 46, 74). Repeats per unit exactly as
                                loadTransferRelation does; see diggs:GeoUnitDesignQuantityType's own
                                documentation for why one instance's populated fields vary by the
                                unit's role in the analysis (shaft-friction layer vs. bearing
                                stratum vs. the layer containing the neutral
                                plane).</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="otherProperty"
                        type="diggs:ParameterPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The dictionary escape hatch, matching the pattern of the
                                same name on diggs:AbstractTargetObjectType: a design-level quantity
                                that has no named element on this type and no more specific
                                dictionary-bound container, carried as a diggs:AuthorizedParameter
                                bound to a definition in ri_design_properties.xml.

                                FORMERLY CARRIED ROWS 18, 31 AND THE GEOMETRY-ENVELOPE PART OF ROW 13
                                OF THE DESIGN ANALYSIS; RELOCATED ON REVIEW. The equivalent/tributary
                                diameter is now the typed equivalentDiameter element above (it is
                                method-agnostic and computed entirely from fields already on this
                                type, so it earns the same typed-convenience treatment as totalArea/
                                cutoffElevation elsewhere in this schema, rather than the dictionary
                                escape hatch). The column axial utilization ratio was retired outright
                                - diggs:DesignVerificationType/utilisationRatio (S15), reachable
                                through this type's own verification array with
                                verificationType=structural_axial, already states exactly this
                                quantity (demand divided by capacity), and the dictionary entry was a
                                second, unreconciled way to say the same thing. The dimensionless
                                geometry and stiffness ratios that gate arching-method applicability
                                (h/(s-d), d/s, sx/sy, ks,T/ks) moved to
                                diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType/methodApplicabilityRatio: unlike the
                                equivalent diameter, none of them is a per-column property at all -
                                each is a judgment about whether a chosen method's assumptions hold
                                for a given zone's whole layout, the same kind of whole-system
                                judgment diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType/verification already houses for
                                global stability, lateral spreading and seismic/liquefaction, even
                                though computed from fields that live on this type and
                                diggs:RILTPDesignType both. See that element's own documentation for
                                the fuller reasoning, including why a specifically-named container
                                rather than bare otherProperty.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="ArchingCoefficients" type="diggs:ArchingCoefficientsType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The coefficients an arching (load-transfer) calculation produced under
                one named model, together with the model itself.

                ONE OBJECT PER MODEL, GENERIC ACROSS ALL OF THEM. BS 8006-1's Marston coefficient
                and cap-stress ratio, the Hewlett-Randolph dome-arching efficiencies and passive
                coefficient, EBGEO's Zaeske critical stress ratio, the adapted-Terzaghi lateral
                earth-pressure coefficient and ASIRI's Prandtl punching factor are all intermediate
                quantities that exist only inside one arching model, that hard-coding as named
                elements would have baked four competing national methods into the DIGGS namespace,
                and that a fifth method (van Eekelen's concentric arches, CUR226) would then have
                required a schema change to add. Route DICT is correct for the coefficients
                themselves; this object is what gives each coefficient somewhere to live and gives
                the dictionary's Occurrence mechanism a local element to condition on.

                ARCHINGMODEL IS REPEATED HERE RATHER THAN READ FROM A BASIS OBJECT, and that is a
                structural necessity rather than a preference: a dictionary Definition's
                conditionalElementXpath is evaluated with the ancestor:: axis, which can only see
                containment, not a basisRef cross-reference. So each coefficient's Occurrence
                condition reads ancestor::diggs:ArchingCoefficients/diggs:archingModel, which is
                only resolvable if the model is stated locally, in the same containment tree as the
                coefficient. A diggs:RIProgramBasis cited through basisRef (S9) may additionally
                carry its own archingModel as the program-level record of record, but this local
                statement remains the authoritative one for the dictionary Occurrence mechanism to
                key on; the two should agree, and repeating the code costs one element against
                having the validation mechanism work at all.

                Repeatable on the design that holds it, for the uncommon case of a design that
                reports coefficients under more than one model for comparison; normally
                one.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="ArchingCoefficientsType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for diggs:ArchingCoefficients. See the element's documentation for
                why this exists as one generic object rather than named elements per
                coefficient.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractComponentObjectBaseType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="1" name="archingModel" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The arching (load-transfer) model these coefficients were
                                computed under - Marston, Hewlett-Randolph, Zaeske, adapted-Terzaghi,
                                Prandtl and so on. Mandatory: a coefficient reported without its model
                                is not interpretable, and this is the element every coefficient's
                                dictionary Occurrence condition keys on. Vocabulary in
                                archingModel.xml.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="coefficient"
                        type="diggs:AuthorizedParameterArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>One or more named coefficients belonging to the model
                                stated above, each an AuthorizedParameter or ProvenancedParameter
                                whose parameterName codeSpace resolves into ri_design_properties.xml.
                                diggs:AuthorizedParameterArrayPropertyType, not
                                diggs:ParameterArrayPropertyType - schema-enforced, not merely
                                documented (an earlier draft relied on this element's own
                                documentation alone, per B2): the whole point of this object is that a
                                consumer can type and validate each coefficient against its dictionary
                                definition rather than infer it from a bare name-value pair, which a
                                plain diggs:Parameter's uncontrolled name would allow to go
                                unchecked.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <complexType name="ArchingCoefficientsPropertyType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Property type for containing a single diggs:ArchingCoefficients
                object.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <sequence>
            <element ref="diggs:ArchingCoefficients" minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1"/>
        </sequence>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RILTPDesign" type="diggs:RILTPDesignType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractProgramDesign">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The design of a load transfer platform: its thickness, the arching
                behaviour it is designed to develop, and the stresses that behaviour delivers to
                the column heads and to the soil between them.

                Normally instance-scoped rather than population-scoped, unlike RIColumnDesign - a
                site has a handful of platforms, not thousands, so scoping one design to one
                platform through appliesToFeatureRef is usually the right form.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RILTPDesignType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the load transfer platform design. Inherits scope, basedOnRef,
                programSpecificationRef and basisRef from
                diggs:AbstractProgramDesignType.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractProgramDesignType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="ltpThickness" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The designed vertical thickness of the load transfer
                                platform.

                                THICKNESS IS NOT ONLY AN ARCHING PARAMETER, which is easy to miss
                                when it is read off a list beside the arching coefficients. It also
                                controls lateral load transfer and column bending: test data show
                                the lateral capacity of a stiff-column-supported footing increasing
                                roughly linearly with platform thickness over the range used in
                                practice. So this value is an input to the column shear and bending
                                checks as much as to the stress distribution below.

                                Compare diggs:LoadTransferPlatformType/impliedLTPThickness on the
                                as-built side, which is inferred from the platform top and working
                                platform elevations rather than stated.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="criticalHeight" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The fill height above which differential settlement no
                                longer reaches the surface - the full-arching height.

                                A UNIVERSAL CONCEPT WITH A MODEL-DEPENDENT VALUE. Every arching
                                practice has this quantity, which is why it is a typed element here
                                rather than a dictionary entry; but what it evaluates to depends
                                entirely on which arching model was used, so the number is not
                                interpretable without that model. State the model on a sibling
                                diggs:archingCoefficients/archingModel on this same design - present
                                whether or not that object carries any coefficients - and read the
                                two together; that is also the element every coefficient's own
                                dictionary Occurrence condition keys on, so it is guaranteed to be
                                locally resolvable in a way a basisRef target is not. The model may
                                additionally be stated on a diggs:RIProgramBasis cited through
                                basisRef (S9), as the program-level record of record; the two should
                                agree.

                                Placed on the platform design rather than the column design although
                                the source parameter analysis groups it with layout: it is an output
                                of the arching calculation, and arching is what the platform is
                                designed to do.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <choice minOccurs="0">
                        <element name="sideSlopeRatio" type="diggs:DimensionlessMeasureType">
                            <annotation>
                                <documentation>The side slope of the embankment above the platform,
                                    expressed as a ratio of horizontal to vertical.

                                    A RATIO AND AN ANGLE ARE DIFFERENT QUANTITY CLASSES, not two
                                    units of one quantity, so they are two elements in a choice
                                    rather than one element with a permissive uom. Collapsing them
                                    would put a plane angle and a dimensionless ratio behind the
                                    same name and leave a consumer to infer which was meant from the
                                    unit string. State whichever form the design was expressed in;
                                    the choice makes stating both impossible, which is the
                                    point.

                                    Applies only where the supported structure is an embankment.</documentation>
                            </annotation>
                        </element>
                        <element name="sideSlopeAngle" type="diggs:PlaneAngleMeasureType">
                            <annotation>
                                <documentation>The side slope of the embankment above the platform,
                                    expressed as an angle from horizontal. See sideSlopeRatio for
                                    why these are two elements rather than one.</documentation>
                            </annotation>
                        </element>
                    </choice>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="embankmentHeight" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>H: the height of the embankment or fill above the
                                platform. Row 76 (S20). Conditional on structure type = embankment,
                                like sideSlopeRatio/sideSlopeAngle above, which it pairs
                                with.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="stressOnElementHead"
                        type="diggs:PressureMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The designed vertical stress delivered to the head of one
                                rigid inclusion through the platform.

                                THIS AND stressOnSoftSoil ARE THE PAIR THAT MAKES DESIGNS COMPARABLE
                                ACROSS PRACTICES, and that is why they are typed elements while the
                                arching coefficients that produce them are not. Every arching model,
                                whatever its internals, ultimately outputs how much stress lands on
                                the head and how much lands on the soil between - the load-transfer
                                decompositions, the dome-arching efficiencies, the cap-stress ratios
                                and the earth-pressure coefficients all resolve to that pair. Give
                                the pair two elements, and everything upstream of them dictionary
                                entries conditioned on the arching model, and a consumer can compare
                                two designs on the two quantities that are actually
                                comparable.

                                The source parameter analysis lists this quantity twice under two
                                symbols; they are one property and are merged
                                here.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="stressOnSoftSoil" type="diggs:PressureMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The designed residual vertical stress reaching the
                                compressible soil between the column heads. The other half of the
                                arching outcome; see stressOnElementHead.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="loadEfficacy" type="diggs:DimensionlessMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>E: the fraction of total load carried by the inclusions.
                                Row 80 (S20). Placed here, with the arching outcome, rather than on
                                RITreatedAreaDesignType: "load efficacy" is defined at least three
                                different ways in the literature - load on column / total load,
                                stress on column / average stress, complement of the soil-borne
                                fraction - and each definition is computed from exactly the
                                stressOnElementHead/stressOnSoftSoil pair above, not from a
                                system-wide quantity. Contrast settlementEfficacy on
                                RITreatedAreaDesignType, a genuinely site-wide serviceability
                                metric.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="loadEfficacyDefinition" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Which of the competing definitions loadEfficacy uses.
                                Mandatory in effect, though optional in XSD terms: Han's 2025 method
                                comparison uses load efficacy as a headline metric precisely because
                                methods disagree on its definition, so a value reported without this
                                code is not comparable across designs.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="archingCoefficients"
                        type="diggs:ArchingCoefficientsPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The coefficients the arching calculation produced, together
                                with the model that produced them. See diggs:ArchingCoefficients for
                                why this is one generic object rather than a named element per
                                coefficient, and criticalHeight above for why archingModel is stated
                                here rather than solely on a program basis.

                                Repeatable for the uncommon case of a design comparing coefficients
                                under more than one model; state one archingModel with no coefficient
                                array at minimum wherever criticalHeight or either stress element is
                                reported, so those values remain interpretable even when no individual
                                coefficient is being carried.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="geosyntheticLayerDesign"
                        type="diggs:GeosyntheticLayerDesignArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The geosynthetic reinforcement layers designed into this
                                platform (Design Parameter Analysis rows 61-69, absorbing 85). One
                                per layer; repeating this element is row 62's layer count, and its
                                presence is row 61's "geosynthetic present" - see
                                diggs:GeosyntheticLayerDesign's own documentation. Compare
                                diggs:LoadTransferPlatformType/geosynthetics, the as-built
                                counterpart.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="activeEarthPressureCoefficient"
                        type="diggs:PressurePerPressureMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Ka = tan^2(45deg - phi'/2): the active earth pressure
                                coefficient of the embankment fill (BS 8006-1:2010+A1:2016),
                                governing lateral thrust and lateral spreading checks (Design
                                Parameter Analysis row 82).

                                A TYPED ELEMENT, NOT DICTIONARY-BOUND, ON FURTHER REVIEW - briefly a
                                diggs:ParameterPropertyType slot named specifically for this quantity
                                (relocated off diggs:otherProperty on the same review pass that
                                produced diggs:RITreatedAreaDesignType/methodApplicabilityRatio), then
                                reconsidered: a single, always-the-same-formula quantity does not
                                need the dictionary machinery at all, the same reasoning that already
                                promoted equivalentDiameter above. The dictionary route earns its
                                keep for a genuinely open vocabulary (methodApplicabilityRatio, where
                                a future arching method may add its own applicability check) or a
                                fixed, closed set of per-model values (diggs:ArchingCoefficients'
                                coefficient); Ka is neither - it is one formula, cited once, the same
                                category as columnDiameter or spacingX.

                                DELIBERATELY NOT IN diggs:ArchingCoefficients alongside the other
                                Group F/I coefficients, and not in methodApplicabilityRatio either:
                                Ka is a property of the FILL, used across whichever arching model
                                governs the vertical load transfer - not an output specific to one
                                model, and not a method-applicability judgment
                                either.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="otherProperty"
                        type="diggs:ParameterPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The dictionary escape hatch, matching
                                diggs:RIColumnDesignType/otherProperty and the same-named pattern on
                                diggs:AbstractTargetObjectType: a platform-design quantity that has no
                                named element on this type and no more specific dictionary-bound
                                container, carried as a diggs:AuthorizedParameter bound to a
                                definition in ri_design_properties.xml. Formerly carried the active
                                earth-pressure coefficient (row 82); see activeEarthPressureCoefficient
                                above, relocated on review.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RIColumnSpecification" type="diggs:RIColumnSpecificationType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractProgramSpecification">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The specification the rigid inclusion columns must satisfy - the
                constraints the work is judged against, as distinct from the design that was
                prepared to satisfy them.

                A THIN TYPE, AND DELIBERATELY SO. Most of what a column specification says is
                limits - position tolerance, verticality tolerance, minimum embedment into the
                bearing stratum, permitted diameter and length ranges - and every one of those is
                a diggs:DesignConformanceCriterion or diggs:PerformanceCriterion declared as a
                sibling under the program's specification slot. A criterion carries an operator, a
                limit with min/value/max and units, a statistic, and a basis that can express
                "two times the diameter" as readily as an absolute length; restating any of that
                as a typed element here would be a second, weaker way to say the same thing.

                What remains on this object is the non-limit content: what technique is permitted
                and what material specification governs. Its other job is to be a single stable
                reference target - a diggs:RIColumn cites it through
                programSpecificationRef, a design cites it through programSpecificationRef, and
                each individual criterion cites it through basedOnRef, which is a within-head
                chain and therefore legal.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RIColumnSpecificationType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the rigid inclusion column specification. Inherits scope and
                basedOnRef from diggs:AbstractProgramModuleType by way of
                diggs:AbstractProgramSpecificationType, which adds nothing of its
                own.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractProgramSpecificationType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="columnType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The rigid inclusion technique family the specification
                                permits or requires - controlled modulus columns, auger cast-in-place,
                                continuous flight auger, vibrated concrete columns, driven precast
                                and so on.

                                Named to match diggs:RIColumnType/columnType, the as-built
                                counterpart, and typed the same way. gml:CodeType with a dictionary
                                vocabulary rather than an enumeration: technique families proliferate
                                under proprietary names and the list cannot be
                                closed.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" ref="diggs:groutSpecificationRef">
                        <annotation>
                            <appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement">diggs:GroutSpecification</appinfo>
                            <documentation>The grout or concrete specification governing the column
                                material - the object that carries strength requirements, permitted
                                deviations and gradation limits.

                                Reuses the global element already declared in
                                specialty/Grouting.xsd rather than declaring an RI-specific
                                equivalent, and cites the existing diggs:GroutSpecification rather
                                than restating a concrete strength class as a code here.
                                diggs:GroutSpecificationType already carries strengthMinimum,
                                strengthAverage and strength90Percent, so a materialClass element on
                                this type would be a lossier duplicate of a richer object that
                                exists.

                                A reference rather than basedOnRef even though both objects are
                                specifications: a material specification is a COMPONENT of the
                                column specification, not a generalization of it, so inheritance
                                would be the wrong relation and would silently pull grout
                                constraints into this object's own resolution
                                chain.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RILTPSpecification" type="diggs:RILTPSpecificationType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractProgramSpecification">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The specification a load transfer platform must satisfy.

                Thinner still than diggs:RIColumnSpecification, and for the same reason: platform
                requirements are overwhelmingly limits - minimum thickness, target relative
                compaction, permitted settlement - and every limit is an acceptance criterion
                declared as a sibling under the program's specification slot, citing this object
                through basedOnRef.

                THE FILL GRADATION ENVELOPE IS CARRIED HERE; TARGET COMPACTION IS NOT. The platform
                fill's gradation and compaction requirements appear in essentially every rigid
                inclusion submittal and drive the post-installation compaction testing, and neither
                this type nor the as-built diggs:LoadTransferPlatformType carried them - the
                as-built has materialType alone. The two halves need different mechanisms and get
                them: target relative compaction is a single limit with an operator, so it is an
                acceptance criterion and needs nothing new here, whereas a gradation envelope is a
                pair of CURVES and cannot be an operator-and-limit at all. The curve pair below
                reuses the shape diggs:GroutSpecificationType already established for exactly this
                problem.

                PROVISIONAL PENDING SME REVIEW (Dan, 2026-08-16): reuse the shape now, confirm the
                fit later.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RILTPSpecificationType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the load transfer platform specification. Inherits scope and
                basedOnRef from diggs:AbstractProgramModuleType by way of
                diggs:AbstractProgramSpecificationType.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractProgramSpecificationType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="materialType" type="diggs:LithologyPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The fill material the platform is specified to be built
                                from. Named to match diggs:LoadTransferPlatformType/materialType,
                                the as-built counterpart, and typed the same way so that specified
                                and constructed material can be compared without a
                                translation.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="platformType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The specified construction type of the load transfer
                                platform (V2 Design row 50). See
                                diggs:LoadTransferPlatformType/platformType, the as-built
                                counterpart, for the full reasoning and the shared
                                vocabulary.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="aggregateGradationCurveCoarseLimit"
                        type="diggs:SieveAnalysisPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The COARSE bound of the permitted gradation envelope for
                                the platform fill - the curve the material may not be coarser than.
                                Together with aggregateGradationCurveFineLimit it defines the band a
                                conforming gradation must fall inside.

                                A GRADATION LIMIT IS A CURVE, NOT A NUMBER, which is why this is not
                                an acceptance criterion like the platform's other requirements. An
                                AcceptanceCriterion carries an operator and a limit with min, value
                                and max, which expresses a scalar band perfectly and a
                                percent-passing curve across a dozen sieve sizes not at all. Target
                                relative compaction IS a scalar band and correctly stays a
                                criterion; this is the requirement that cannot be.

                                THE SHAPE IS BORROWED FROM diggs:GroutSpecificationType, which
                                carries an identically named pair for the aggregate in a grout mix -
                                one concept, one name, one shape, in the only other place in DIGGS
                                that had this problem. The object type is not borrowed:
                                diggs:SieveAnalysisPropertyType is the generic core type, used in
                                preference to Grouting's GroutGradingSpecificationPropertyType,
                                which is a bare extension of diggs:SieveAnalysisType adding nothing
                                at all and whose name would misdescribe a crushed-stone platform
                                fill. Reusing the generic type takes the shared shape without
                                importing a grout-specific name or a needless cross-specialty
                                dependency.

                                Provisional pending SME confirmation that the envelope is the right
                                expression of the requirement - see this element's parent
                                documentation.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="aggregateGradationCurveFineLimit"
                        type="diggs:SieveAnalysisPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The FINE bound of the permitted gradation envelope for
                                the platform fill - the curve the material may not be finer than.
                                The other half of the band; see
                                aggregateGradationCurveCoarseLimit for why a curve pair rather than
                                an acceptance criterion.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- C2.1 — RI PROGRAM BASIS (S9)                                  -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- The third module kind alongside C2's designs and specifications - the evidence and
         governing method a design was prepared from, as distinct from what is intended (design)
         or what is required (specification). diggs:AbstractProgramBasis (core/AbstractTypes.xsd)
         has carried zero concrete members since it was declared; this is the first.

         ONE TYPE, NOT A GroundModelBasis / LoadingBasis / RequirementsBasis split. An earlier
         architecture sketch (RI_Program_Architecture.md §6) proposed three basis subtypes keyed by
         kind of evidence. What Design Parameter Analysis Group A (rows 2-13) actually calls for is
         narrower and does not split cleanly along that boundary - riTechniqueType and archingModel
         are method statements, not loading or ground-model evidence, and would sit awkwardly in
         any of the three baskets. A single RIProgramBasis, parallel to RITreatedAreaDesign, carries
         every Group A field this pass builds; splitting further is deferred until a second
         concrete basis type actually needs to disagree with this one on scope, which would be the
         reason to separate them.

         GOVERNING STANDARD REUSES diggs:Specification, NOT A NEW TYPE. RI_Design_Parameter_
         Analysis.md row 2 confirms diggs:SpecificationPropertyType (core/Common.xsd) already
         carries everything a citation needs (standardReferenceNumber, standardTitle,
         standardVersion, standardPart, standardClause, accredtingBody, shortMethodName) -
         unbounded here so a multi-standard project (ASIRI + EC7 + a project spec) is expressible
         with one clause each.

         ROW 11 (the partial/global factor set) IS NOT HERE. It needs S13's DesignFactor object,
         which does not exist yet; the coded fields land first per the plan's own dependency note.
         Additive once S13 is built - a new optional element, no restructuring.

         ROW 12 (parameter provenance) and the geometric-ratio half of ROW 13 ARE ALSO NOT HERE -
         both already have a home: row 12 is diggs:ProvenancedParameter (F2, B4), and row 13's
         ratios are RIColumnDesignType/otherProperty (S5). ROW 6 (delivery/contracting method) is
         out of scope - a Contract-level gap noted in the analysis, not RI-specific.

         archingModel AND riTechniqueType DELIBERATELY REUSE ELEMENT NAMES ALREADY IN USE ELSEWHERE
         (diggs:ArchingCoefficients/archingModel, S5; diggs:RIColumnType/columnType and
         diggs:RIColumnSpecificationType/columnType, S6) SO THE SAME DICTIONARY GOVERNS BOTH. Both
         remain optional here: the design-side, locally-resolvable statements
         (archingCoefficients/archingModel; columnType on the specification or as-built) are what a
         consumer can actually resolve without following basisRef, and this object is the
         program-level record of record the two should agree with, not the sole source. -->
    <element name="RIProgramBasis" type="diggs:RIProgramBasisType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractProgramBasis">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The site-wide basis a rigid inclusion program's designs were prepared
                from: the governing standards, design method, supported structure type, technique
                family, arching model, safety format, working life and consequence class that make
                every other design value in the program interpretable. Cited by a design through
                basisRef, parallel to how diggs:RITreatedAreaDesign is cited through
                basedOnRef.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RIProgramBasisType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the site-wide rigid inclusion program basis. Inherits scope and
                basedOnRef from diggs:AbstractProgramModuleType by way of
                diggs:AbstractProgramBasisType, which adds nothing of its own.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractProgramBasisType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="governingStandard"
                        type="diggs:SpecificationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A standard or recommendation the design was prepared
                                under - ASIRI, EC7, BS 8006-1, EBGEO, a project specification.
                                Repeatable: a multi-national or hybrid-method project may cite
                                several, each with its own edition and clause via the referenced
                                diggs:Specification's standardVersion and standardClause. No new
                                type - diggs:SpecificationPropertyType already carries everything a
                                citation needs (RI_Design_Parameter_Analysis.md Group A row
                                2).</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="designMethod" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The analytical approach the design was calculated by -
                                unit-cell method, arching (limit-equilibrium or numerical), finite
                                element analysis, load-displacement-compatibility method, or
                                other.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="1" name="supportedStructureType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>What the rigid inclusions support - footing, mat, slab,
                                embankment, wall, tank, culvert, bridge. MANDATORY: this is the
                                master conditional a large part of the Design and Post-installation
                                groups key on - side slope, embankment height, critical height,
                                lateral spreading and most of the embankment-specific group are
                                meaningful only where this names an embankment or wall, while slab
                                stress concentration is meaningful only where it names a slab or
                                mat. A consumer needs this value to know which other elements should
                                even be present.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="riTechniqueType" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The rigid inclusion technique family the program's
                                designs assume - the same vocabulary and dictionary as
                                diggs:RIColumnType/columnType and
                                diggs:RIColumnSpecificationType/columnType (S6), so a technique
                                family stated at the program level and one stated per column or per
                                specification agree by construction rather than by convention. State
                                the more specific element (columnType, on the specification or
                                as-built) where it may vary; this one is the program-wide
                                default.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="archingModel" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The load-transfer (arching) model the program's designs
                                assume, drawn from the same dictionary as
                                diggs:ArchingCoefficients/archingModel (S5). That sibling element on
                                the design itself, not this one, is what every arching coefficient's
                                own dictionary Occurrence condition keys on, since a
                                conditionalElementXpath cannot reach across basisRef - see
                                RILTPDesignType/criticalHeight and /archingCoefficients for why.
                                This element is the program-level record of record the design-level
                                statement should agree with.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="safetyFormat" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The design approach and partial-factor framework applied
                                - Eurocode 7 Design Approach 2 or 3, BS limit state design, DIN load
                                cases, or an allowable-stress method with a global factor of safety.
                                Governs how the values reported elsewhere in the design are meant to
                                be checked.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="designWorkingLife" type="diggs:TimeMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The service life the design assumes - governs the
                                consequence class, durability provisions and, where the safety
                                format is Eurocode-based, which partial factors
                                apply.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="consequenceClass" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The consequence or reliability class governing the
                                design - for example a Eurocode consequence class (EN 1990
                                CC1-CC3) or an equivalent national scheme. RI_Design_Parameter_
                                Analysis.md Group A row 10 flags that several national schemes
                                (ASIRI's domain classification, EBGEO's load case) use this same
                                slot for concepts that are not directly interchangeable with each
                                other or with the Eurocode classes; the dictionary vocabulary for
                                those is intentionally left for SME confirmation rather than
                                guessed at, so this element's published vocabulary is EC7/EN
                                1990-sourced only as of this build.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="factor"
                        type="diggs:DesignFactorPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The partial or global factor set this basis's safety
                                format applies - Design Parameter Analysis Group A row 11. Repeating,
                                one diggs:DesignFactor per named factor. Left out of this type's
                                initial build (S9) because diggs:DesignFactor did not exist yet;
                                added here once S13 built it - a pure addition, no restructuring of
                                anything S9 shipped.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- C3 — THE RI CONSTRUCTION ACTIVITY                            -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- The real-time telemetry record for constructing one column - drilling and, where the
         method injects grout during withdrawal, grouting - as a diggs:AbstractConstructionActivity,
         following the precedent §9 note 8 and architecture's phase-3 correction both point at:
         PileDrivingActivityType (specialty/DeepFoundation.xsd) - scalar summary properties plus a
         depth- or time-indexed COVERAGE for the series data, not scalars alone. The commented block
         C4 retired (CMCConstructionActivityType) modeled torqueApplied, penetrationPerRevolution,
         penetrationRate, withdrawalRate, rotationSpeeed (sic), groutPressure and groutFlowRate all
         as SCALARS - one value per activity - which cannot represent what a drill rig's data logger
         actually produces: a continuous stream against depth or time. All seven become coverage
         PROPERTY CLASSES here instead, dictionary-bound, per the same logic C2 applied to the
         measurement-plan properties: a value that varies continuously needs a place to vary, and a
         single typed element cannot be that place.

         REUSE THE COVERAGE MECHANISM, NOT diggs:MWDResult ITSELF (Dan, 2026-08-16). Core.xsd already
         has exactly the right shape for this - MWDResultType is a restriction of
         gml:AbstractCoverageType choosing a depth (location) or time (timeDomain) domain, paired
         with a diggs:results range whose PropertyParameters/Property/propertyClass values are
         dictionary-bound and open-ended, not a fixed column list. But MWDResult is the OUTCOME of a
         diggs:MeasurementWhileDrilling MEASUREMENT (substituting diggs:AbstractMeasurement, living
         in the <measurement> slot, cited via mwd_properties.xml's own Occurrence condition
         "ancestor::diggs:measurement//diggs:procedure/diggs:MWDProcedure") - an act of MEASURING,
         dedicated to drilling. An RI construction activity is a different abstract head entirely
         (diggs:AbstractConstructionActivity, the <constructionActivity> slot) recording an act of
         BUILDING, of which drilling is only the first half. Embedding MWDResult inside a
         construction activity would misuse an object whose identity is bound to being a
         measurement's outcome, so C3 declares diggs:RIInstallationRecordType as its own
         restriction of gml:AbstractCoverageType, IDENTICAL IN SHAPE to MWDResultType - same choice,
         same results particle - but its own name and its own object, correctly scoped to the
         construction-activity head. The shape is borrowed; the object is not, by direct instruction
         rather than by the reasoning C2 used for the same move on aggregateGradationCurve.

         ONE STREAM, MIXED COLUMNS, TWO EXISTING DICTIONARIES, ZERO NEW ENTRIES FOR SIX OF SEVEN.
         "Covering both drilling and grouting" does not need two coverage types: diggs:results'
         PropertyParameters is an open list of Property definitions, so one ResultSet can carry
         drilling columns and grouting columns side by side, exactly as a rig that grouts through the
         auger stem during withdrawal would log them - one continuous depth-indexed stream. Checked
         against the two dictionaries already live in the def repo:

           torqueApplied            -> mwd_properties.xml#torque / gr_inj_properties.xml#torque
           penetrationRate          -> mwd_properties.xml#penetration_rate (or #drilling_speed)
           withdrawalRate           -> gr_inj_properties.xml#pull_speed
           rotationSpeeed (sic)     -> mwd_properties.xml#rotation_shaft / gr_inj#rotation_speed
           groutPressure            -> gr_inj_properties.xml#grout_pressure
           groutFlowRate            -> gr_inj_properties.xml#grout_flow
           penetrationPerRevolution -> NO MATCH - the one genuine gap, see docs/build/C3/NOTES.md

         Six of the seven retired properties already have a home; nothing needed adding to either
         dictionary for them. Not one new element was needed on either coverage type either - the
         PropertyParameters/dataValues machinery built for MWD already does exactly this job. -->
    <element name="RIInstallationRecord" type="diggs:RIInstallationRecordType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>A depth- or time-indexed record of the real-time telemetry produced while
                constructing one rigid inclusion column - drilling parameters, grouting parameters,
                or both mixed in one stream, according to what the producing system actually logs
                together. Identical in shape to diggs:MWDResultType (a restriction of
                gml:AbstractCoverageType choosing a location or timeDomain and carrying a
                diggs:results range) but its own object, scoped to the construction-activity head
                rather than to a measurement - see the header comment above
                diggs:RIConstructionActivityType for why the two are not the same
                object.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RIInstallationRecordType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Restricted type based on gml:AbstractCoverageType, mirroring
                diggs:MWDResultType particle for particle. Where location carries the domain, expect
                a diggs:LinearExtent in the column's own linear referencing system marking the depth
                interval the record spans - the same idiom diggs:RIConstructionMethodType/location
                already uses for its own, coarser intervals. Where timeDomain carries it instead, the
                per-row depth (if wanted) travels as its own property column in results, exactly as
                "Measured Depth" does in the worked diggs-examples MWD instance. results carries an
                open PropertyParameters list, so drilling columns (propertyClass values from
                mwd_properties.xml) and grouting columns (from gr_inj_properties.xml) may sit in the
                same row set.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <restriction base="gml:AbstractCoverageType">
                <sequence>
                    <choice>
                        <element ref="diggs:location"/>
                        <element ref="diggs:timeDomain"/>
                    </choice>
                    <element ref="diggs:results"/>
                </sequence>
            </restriction>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
    <complexType name="RIInstallationRecordPropertyType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Property type for RIInstallationRecord. Carries an index attribute,
                mirroring diggs:PileDrivingRecordPropertyType, so that where a producer's system
                emits drilling and grouting as separate records (rather than one mixed stream) their
                relative sequence is stated rather than left to document
                order.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <sequence minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="1">
            <element ref="diggs:RIInstallationRecord"/>
        </sequence>
        <attribute name="index" type="positiveInteger">
            <annotation>
                <documentation>The sequence position of this record where more than one is present -
                    for example, 1 for a drilling-phase record and 2 for the grouting-phase record
                    that follows it during withdrawal.</documentation>
            </annotation>
        </attribute>
    </complexType>
    <element name="RIConstructionActivity" type="diggs:RIConstructionActivityType"
        substitutionGroup="diggs:AbstractConstructionActivity">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The act of constructing one rigid inclusion column: drilling, and, where
                the method grouts through the auger stem, grouting. Carries the real-time telemetry
                record; diggs:RIConstructionMethodType on the diggs:RIColumn feature carries
                the summary derived from it. Where the two disagree, THIS RECORD GOVERNS.

                Scoped to column construction only. Load transfer platform construction is
                compaction, not drilling or grouting, and is out of C3's scope - the retired property
                list this step inherited (torqueApplied, penetrationRate, withdrawalRate,
                rotationSpeeed, groutPressure, groutFlowRate) names nothing platform-related, and
                nothing in the plan or architecture asked for an LTP construction
                activity.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>
    <complexType name="RIConstructionActivityType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the rigid inclusion construction activity. Inherits
                investigationTarget, projectRef, the mandatory samplingFeatureRef, programRef,
                programSpecificationRef, constructionMethod, constructionEnvironment, equipment,
                otherConstructionActivityProperty and activityDateTime from
                diggs:AbstractConstructionActivityType.

                THE INHERITED samplingFeatureRef MUST TARGET A diggs:RIColumn FOR THIS TYPE,
                AND XSD CANNOT SAY SO LOCALLY. Its global element already carries a broad appinfo
                (widened to diggs:AbstractSamplingFeature in C1 item 5) and the particle is declared
                on the ABSTRACT base type, not here - extension can only ADD particles after an
                inherited sequence, not narrow one already declared by the type being
                extended, so there is no XSD position left to hang a local, narrower
                annotation on. Stated here in prose instead, and left for the reference-validation
                module's context check (architecture §12) to enforce
                structurally.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractConstructionActivityType">
                <sequence>
                    <element minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" name="installationRecord"
                        type="diggs:RIInstallationRecordPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The real-time telemetry record of this construction
                                activity - see the header comment above this type and the one above
                                diggs:RIInstallationRecordType for the reasoning behind its shape.

                                Unbounded, mirroring diggs:PileDrivingActivityType's
                                pileDrivingRecord/pdaRecord pair: a producer may supply one mixed
                                drilling-and-grouting stream, or separate records per phase or per
                                equipment system, at their own discretion. Nothing here forces a
                                single row set.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="terminationOutcome" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The achieved reason this column's installation stopped -
                                for example design depth reached, or refusal (Installation Parameter
                                Analysis row 40, S21). An as-built fact, distinct from the refusal
                                criterion (row 39) that governs the stop decision: the criterion is
                                a rule and belongs on the specification side as a B5 acceptance
                                criterion cited by this activity; this element is the outcome that
                                rule produced. See Installation Parameter Analysis G6 for the full
                                reasoning behind the split, including the open question that B5's
                                single-operator AbstractAcceptanceCriterionType cannot express a
                                compound rate-and-torque refusal rule as written - not addressed
                                here.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="toeEmbedment" type="diggs:LengthMeasureType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>As-built embedment of the column toe into the competent
                                (bearing) stratum (Installation Parameter Analysis row 41, S21).
                                In principle derivable by crossing the installationRecord coverage's
                                depth column, or diggs:RIColumn/centerLine, against a ground
                                model - but only where a ground model is present in the same file,
                                which an ITP recording this value at the rig cannot assume. Pairs
                                with the design-side minimum required embedment, a B5 acceptance
                                criterion (Design Parameter Analysis row 45).</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <choice minOccurs="0">
                        <element name="embedmentLayer" type="string">
                            <annotation>
                                <documentation>The name of the ground model layer that the column terminates in. This element is to be used, instead of embedmentLayerRef, where a GeoUnitSystem used to represent the ground model is not reachable from this instance, or where a formal ground model was not developed.</documentation>
                            </annotation>
                        </element>
                        <element name="embedmentLayerRef" type="diggs:FeatureReferenceType">
                            <annotation>
                                <appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement">diggs:GeoUnitObservation</appinfo>
                                <documentation>An xlink:href pointer to the GeoUnitObservation layer from the ground model where termination occurred. Use of this element assumes that the GeoUnitSystem that represents the ground model for this site is reachable from this instance.</documentation>
                            </annotation>
                        </element>
                    </choice>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="installationMethod" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The soil-displacement classification of the installation
                                method actually used - full-displacement auger, partial-displacement,
                                drilled-displacement, or pre-drill/spoil-removed (Installation
                                Parameter Analysis row 7, S22, decision D5).

                                DISTINCT FROM diggs:RIColumnType/columnType, and deliberately
                                its own element rather than a reuse. columnType names the RIG/
                                TECHNIQUE FAMILY (bored, driven, vibro-driven, soil mixing, jet
                                grouting, ...) - see columnType.xml's eleven entries. installationMethod
                                names the DISPLACEMENT BEHAVIOUR of that technique - whether it
                                pushes soil aside, removes it, or something between - a property
                                that matters independently of technique family (a full-displacement
                                auger and a partial-displacement auger are both "auger" columnTypes
                                but change the surrounding ground very differently) and is not
                                derivable from columnType alone. D5 (RI_Build_Plan_2.md) confirmed a
                                distinct coded element rather than treating columnType as
                                sufficient.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="groutLevelCorrectiveAction" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The corrective action taken where in-progress monitoring
                                found the grout level in the column had dropped below the expected
                                level (V2 Installation row 31: grout-level monitoring / grout drop,
                                an "uncovered column" condition) - topping off, or re-drilling and
                                re-grouting the column.

                                THE OUTCOME OF A SITE CHECK, NOT THE CHECK ITSELF. Where the
                                depth-indexed level reading that triggered this action is also
                                logged, it travels through installationRecord like any other rig
                                telemetry (gr_inj_properties.xml#grout_level), the same relationship
                                terminationOutcome above has to the refusal criterion that triggers
                                it.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>
</schema>
